Q: Writing into ODS file with AODL C# A: <p>Looked a bit at some libs, including AODL. Some are old and don't target new runtimes, some are paid/licenced, some are bugged.</p>
<p>One lib I found which you can use to get the job done is <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/OdsReaderWriter" rel="nofollow noreferrer">OdsReaderWriter</a>. You can do most of the work using <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.data.dataset?view=net-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">System.Data.DataSet</a> and <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.data.datatable?view=net-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">System.Data.DataTable</a> to get your data going and just use the lib above to write to the file.</p>
<p>Here's a working example using the code from the DataTable docs:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>static void Main(string[] args)
{
    FileInfo ods = new FileInfo(@&quot;E:\file.ods&quot;);

    //https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.data.datatable?view=net-10.0
    DataTable orderTable = CreateOrderTable();
    DataTable orderDetailTable = CreateOrderDetailTable();

    DataSet salesSet = new();
    salesSet.Tables.Add(orderTable);
    salesSet.Tables.Add(orderDetailTable);

    // Set the relations between the tables
    // and create the related constraint.
    salesSet.Relations.Add(
        &quot;OrderOrderDetail&quot;,
        orderTable.Columns[&quot;OrderId&quot;],
        orderDetailTable.Columns[&quot;OrderId&quot;],
        true);

    InsertOrders(orderTable);
    InsertOrderDetails(orderDetailTable);

    // Use the Aggregate-Sum on the child table column to get the result.
    DataColumn colSub = new(&quot;SubTotal&quot;, typeof(decimal), &quot;Sum(Child.LineTotal)&quot;);
    orderTable.Columns.Add(colSub);

    // Compute the tax by referencing the SubTotal expression column.
    DataColumn colTax = new(&quot;Tax&quot;, typeof(decimal), &quot;SubTotal*0.1&quot;);
    orderTable.Columns.Add(colTax);

    // If the OrderId is 'Total', compute the amount due on all orders; otherwise, compute the amount due on this order.
    DataColumn colTotal = new(
        &quot;TotalDue&quot;,
        typeof(decimal),
        &quot;IIF(OrderId='Total',Sum(SubTotal)+Sum(Tax),SubTotal+Tax)&quot;);
    orderTable.Columns.Add(colTotal);

    DataRow row = orderTable.NewRow();
    row[&quot;OrderId&quot;] = &quot;Total&quot;;
    orderTable.Rows.Add(row);

    //https://www.nuget.org/packages/OdsReaderWriter
    var odsReaderWriter = new ODSReaderWriter();
    Encoding.RegisterProvider(CodePagesEncodingProvider.Instance); //Needed otherwise the lib throws an exception about encodings.
    odsReaderWriter.WriteOdsFile(salesSet, ods.FullName);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Also depending on the scope of your work, if you don't need much, you could always just roll your own. ODF format spec is public and it's basically a zipped xml document. Here's a <a href="https://www.codeguru.com/csharp/how-to-read-and-write-odf-ods-files-opendocument-spreadsheets/#reading-spreadsheet-document" rel="nofollow noreferrer">guide</a> for that.</p>
<p>That said, I'm sure there's likely a better lib out there, but it'll need some looking/toying around.</p>

Q: How to Make a Basic FPS Counter? A: <p>This <a href="https://github.com/lndpj/fps" rel="nofollow noreferrer">fps</a> solution prints time, frame count and frames per second and has non-blocking terminal keyboard io support. Probably using a monotonic POSIX timer signal might be an alternative. Right now it updates from frame count every second.</p>
<pre><code>namespace sys
{
        struct timer
        {
                struct date
                {
                        std::time_t now = 0;
                        char str[32];
                } date;
                struct fps
                {
                        std::size_t cnt = 0;
                        char str[32];
                } fps;
                char str[64];
                using clock = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock;
                std::chrono::time_point&lt;clock&gt; pre = clock::now();
                std::chrono::time_point&lt;clock&gt; now = clock::now();
                std::size_t acc = 0;
                std::size_t dif = std::chrono::duration_cast&lt;std::chrono::nanoseconds&gt;(now - pre).count();

                bool step(const std::size_t len = 1000000000)
                {
                        static bool first = true;
                        pre  = now;
                        now  = clock::now();
                        dif  = std::chrono::duration_cast&lt;std::chrono::nanoseconds&gt;(now - pre).count();
                        acc += dif;

                        if(first)
                        {
                                snprintf(fps.str,31, &quot;%zu&quot;, fps.cnt);
                                date.now = std::chrono::system_clock::to_time_t(now);
                                ctime_r(&amp;date.now, date.str); date.str[strlen(date.str) - 1] = '\0';
                        }
                        if(acc &gt;= len)
                        {
                                snprintf(fps.str,31, &quot;%zu&quot;, fps.cnt);
                                date.now = std::chrono::system_clock::to_time_t(now);
                                ctime_r(&amp;date.now, date.str); date.str[strlen(date.str) - 1] = '\0';
                                fps.cnt = 0;
                                acc -= len;
                                if(first) first = false;
                        }
                        else
                                fps.cnt++;
                        return true;
                }
                const char* c_str()
                {
                        snprintf(str, 64, &quot;%s %8zu/%s&quot;, date.str, fps.cnt, fps.str);
                        return str;
                }
        } timer;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>Tue Jul 14 23:59:51 2026   828982/1199844
</code></pre>
